A||||8||PAUL88GT||Spare parts and tools for travel?||||1||
Z||000000||PAUL88GT||05-02-2000||12:31 PM||||I plan to do some travel in my high mileage (135k miles) 88gt this summer (FOCOA, Run-for-the-Hills etc). What spare parts do you carry for those "unexpected breakdowns" that could leave you stranded on the highway?<P>I am a descent shade tree mechanic and do most simple repairs and maintenance myself, but I am relatively new to Fieros. Also, what tools do you carry? TIA<BR>Paul ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Gold-86SE||05-02-2000||12:50 PM||||I'd carry extra belts, enough tools to cover the minor repair work (like your 10mm, 13mm, and 15mm sockets, 3/8" ratchet, wrenches, etc. etc.) Take an extra quart or two of oil, exta water/coolant, and rags.<P>I'd do a major tuneup prior to riding on the trail. Of course, if you did a recent major tuneup this may not be necessary. Major tuneup being replacing plugs, plug wires, distributer cap and rotor, check timing, pcv valve, air cleaner, o2 sensor, change oil and oil filter, and replace drive belts (keeping the old ones for the trip).<P>Also check your coolant hoses, if they are swollen or look worn replace them.  It is always easier to replace coolant hoses in the garage/driveway than on the road.<P>Also check the tire condition and have the alignment checked.<P>Preventive maintenance is the best hope for an enjoyable trip.  Take the headaches before hand, instead of having a migrane(sp) on the road.||||reg||7
Z||000002||Old Lar||05-02-2000||12:59 PM||||Fuses, more fuses.<P>Air pump for blowing up slow leaky tires.<P>Fix-a-flat.<P>A cell phone to call AAA. (CB radio?)<P>I've needed them all at some time or other or wished I had them in the past.||||reg||1
Z||000003||PAUL88GT||05-02-2000||01:41 PM||||What about the "pick-up coil" or some distributor component that has failed leaving the car dead on the highway? I have heard about this item a few times.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||05-02-2000||05:49 PM||||BFH||||reg||1
Z||000005||mshill||05-02-2000||06:30 PM||||I always carry and extra ignition module and a long phillips screwdriver (for distributor) and a hex driver (6mm?) for the module screws.<P>If your pickup coil has less than 30,000 miles on it I wouldn't worry about it, but it isn't something I would be changing on the side of the road anyway.  I have had such a bad time with ignition modules, that everytime I go to the pick-n-pull I grab a couple to add to my collection just in case I need a temporary fix.<P>I used to carry a flat blade screwdriver and a sharp knife to fix leaky hoses and clamps, but I have replaced all the coolant lines so I don't need them anymore.<P><p>[This message has been edited by mshill (edited 05-02-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Phil||05-02-2000||06:51 PM||||Cell phone and a Visa card?||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||05-02-2000||11:08 PM||||Vise-grips, don't leave home without it.||||reg||1
Z||000008||PAUL88GT||05-02-2000||11:12 PM||||Monkey:<BR>Could you be more specific? Is it a large sexual mallet?||||reg||1
